<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hedwig Jagiellon, Duchess of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1457">1457</a>–<a href="/years/1502">1502</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hedwig Jagiellon was a Polish princess, a daughter of King Casimir IV, who married Duke George the Rich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a> in 1475. Their wedding at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> was one of the most lavish festivities of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and is re-enacted every four years as the Landshut Wedding. She spent much of her later life at the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghausen">Burghausen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hedwig-jagiellon-duchess-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Hedwig Jagiellon, Duchess of Bavaria" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jadwiga_Jagiellonka.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hedwig was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 21 September 1457, the eldest daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV of Poland</a> and Archduchess Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1468 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, asked for her hand, hoping to win an ally against his rival for the Bohemian throne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-podebrady">George of Poděbrady</a>, and he was backed by Emperor Frederick III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-ii">Pope Paul II</a>. But Casimir had been allied with George since 1462, and the queen opposed the match outright. Matthias asked again in 1469, 1471 and 1473 before a final refusal.</p><p>To guard his south-western border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, Casimir sought a closer alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. After talks at Łęczyca and Radom, Hedwig agreed on 30 December 1474 to marry George, son and heir of Duke Louis IX of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a>, with a dowry of 32,000 Hungarian złoty. Because the couple were closely related, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> granted a dispensation on 26 May 1475.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hedwig left her family at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a> on 10 October 1475 with a retinue of about 1,200 knights and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> on 14 November. The wedding was held that day at St. Martin's Church, conducted by Bernhard von Rohr, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, before Emperor Frederick III, his son Maximilian and many German princes, and 9,000 people attended the feast the next day.</p><p>She became duchess-consort in 1479 on the death of her father-in-law. The marriage was unhappy: her husband was known as a drinker and a womaniser, and in 1485 he sent her away from the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> to Burghausen Castle, where she lived with her own court and servants but, according to contemporary chroniclers, deprived of all the pleasures of the world. The couple had five children, among them Elisabeth, who married Rupert, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and Margaret, later abbess at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg an der Donau</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hedwig died unexpectedly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghausen">Burghausen</a> on 18 February 1502 and was buried in the Cistercian monastery of Raitenhaslach; her tombstone was destroyed when the monastery was secularised in 1803. She kept little contact with her family, and on 31 January 1503 her brother, King Alexander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, wrote to her husband that he had not known she had died eleven months before.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hedwig Jagiellon, Duchess of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Louis IX founded the University of Ingolstadt in 1472, and the wedding of George to <b>Hedwig Jagiellon</b> in 1475 was among the most lavish festivals of the age.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-landshut">Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Burghausen, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1486" aria-label="Kraków on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5007 422 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hedwig Jagiellon, Duchess of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1486">What was here in 1486? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
